WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.926 --> 00:00:02.701
- [Voiceover] This is Cruise.

00:00:02.701 --> 00:00:04.451
He's a Malayan tapir,

00:00:05.568 --> 00:00:06.985
and he is hungry.

00:00:08.121 --> 00:00:10.704
(upbeat music)

00:00:13.672 --> 00:00:17.109
Malayan tapirs are the largest
of the four species of tapir,

00:00:17.109 --> 00:00:19.724
and are the only one native to Asia.

00:00:19.724 --> 00:00:22.078
A close relative of the
horse and rhinoceros,

00:00:22.078 --> 00:00:23.998
tapirs have poor eyesight,

00:00:23.998 --> 00:00:26.998
but their sense of hearing
and smell are finely tuned.

00:00:26.998 --> 00:00:28.694
If threatened or frightened,

00:00:28.694 --> 00:00:29.983
they've been known to demonstrate

00:00:29.983 --> 00:00:32.400
pretty quick bursts of speed.

00:00:33.257 --> 00:00:35.054
They are also excellent swimmers,

00:00:35.054 --> 00:00:37.706
and will seek refuge from
predators in deep water

00:00:37.706 --> 00:00:41.442
while using their flexible
nose as a snorkel.

00:00:41.442 --> 00:00:42.977
Although they are vegetarians,

00:00:42.977 --> 00:00:45.172
they can also defend
themselves with a bite

00:00:45.172 --> 00:00:47.589
from their razor-sharp teeth.

00:00:49.109 --> 00:00:50.671
Their distinctive black and white coat

00:00:50.671 --> 00:00:51.905
looks very smart,

00:00:51.905 --> 00:00:55.391
and also serves as an
excellent camouflage.

00:00:55.391 --> 00:00:58.066
Predators will often mistake
a tapir for a large rock

00:00:58.066 --> 00:01:00.693
when they lie down to sleep.

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:02.228
Despite these adaptations,

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:05.006
tapirs are extremely threatened.

00:01:05.006 --> 00:01:07.753
Deforestation and encroaching
human development,

00:01:07.753 --> 00:01:10.064
like flooding from hydroelectric projects

00:01:10.064 --> 00:01:12.989
are all threats to
their shrinking habitat.

00:01:12.989 --> 00:01:15.239
This is the Malayan tapir.

